<DocsLayout title="Push notifications" activeNav="/platform/push-notifications">
  <h1>Push notifications</h1>
  <p>
    A debug tool for sending a push to real devices through Expo Push Service or Firebase Cloud
    Messaging, and reading each provider's response per recipient. It is for answering "did the
    provider accept this token, and if not, why" — not for delivering product notifications.
  </p>

  <Screenshot
    src="push-notifications"
    alt="Push notifications tool with provider tabs, recipient token field, message payload fields and a send panel"
    caption="The tool is labelled DEBUG TOOL for a reason: it sends to real devices."
  />

  <h2>Choosing a provider</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Provider</th><th>Tokens</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Expo Push Service</td>
        <td><code>ExponentPushToken[...]</code></td>
        <td>Fields match Expo's own push tester, so payloads carry over directly. An access token is needed only when Expo push security is enabled.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Firebase (FCM)</td>
        <td>FCM registration tokens</td>
        <td>Sends over FCM HTTP v1, using the Firebase credentials stored for the workspace.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Firebase sending relies on a service account in the
    <a href="/platform/credentials-vault">Credentials Vault</a>. Without one the FCM tab has nothing
    to authenticate with, which is the usual reason a send fails before reaching a device.
  </p>

  <h2>Recipients</h2>
  <p>
    Up to 100 device tokens per send, pasted as a comma-, space-, or line-separated list. The counter
    on the right of the field is the honest check that your paste parsed the way you expected —
    a list that reads as one token shows <code>1/100</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>The payload</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Field</th><th>Purpose</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Message title, body</td><td>What the device shows</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Data</td><td>A JSON object delivered alongside the message, for the app to act on</td></tr>
      <tr><td>TTL</td><td>How long the provider may keep trying, in seconds. Blank uses the provider default.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Priority</td><td>Provider priority hint; the default is usually right</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    <strong>Data</strong> must be a JSON object. It is the field worth using when you are debugging deep
    links or in-app routing, since it is what the app receives rather than what the user sees.
  </p>

  <h2>Reading the response</h2>
  <p>
    Responses appear per recipient rather than as one summary, and that is the point of the tool: a send
    can succeed for one token and fail for the next, and only a per-token result tells you which.
    Each entry carries the provider's own status and message, so an expired token, an unregistered
    device, and a malformed payload are distinguishable instead of all reading as "failed".
  </p>

  <h2>What this tool is not</h2>
  <p>
    It sends to real devices immediately — there is no dry-run — so treat a production token list
    accordingly. It also has no API or CLI equivalent, and no scheduling: it is a console tool for
    diagnosing delivery, not a channel for shipping notifications to users.
  </p>
  <p>
    For build and release notifications to a team, use
    <a href="/platform/integrations/notifications">notification destinations</a> or
    <a href="/platform/integrations/webhooks">webhooks</a> instead.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
